diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that web0 / web0.local is unreachable from host_1_1_1_1 — does not exist in the live lab at the time of this investigation. Every layer of the data path was probed and found fully operational.

Layer 3: host_1_1_1_1 (10.1.1.2) can ping web_server_0 (10.200.0.3) with zero packet loss over the full routed path through switch_dist_1_1, router_core_1, router_core_3, and switch_server_access. DNS: the dns_server (BIND, 10.200.0.2) holds an authoritative zone for "local" and returns the correct A record `web0 → 10.200.0.3`; resolution from host_1_1_1_1 works without error. HTTP: Apache 2.4.62 on web_server_0 is running and listening on 0.0.0.0:80; a live `curl http://web0.local/` issued directly from host_1_1_1_1 returns `HTTP 200 OK` with the expected HTML body in under 5 ms. No iptables rules are present on either host_1_1_1_1 or web_server_0, so there is no access-control filter that could explain intermittent blocking.

Root cause: the ticket describes a transient condition (possibly a brief server restart or temporary network blip earlier in the morning) that had already self-resolved before this investigation began; the infrastructure is currently healthy in all respects.

Corrective fix: no configuration change is required. If the user experiences the issue again, they should clear their browser cache (to rule out a stale connection hang) and report immediately so a real-time packet capture can confirm whether the fault re-emerges.
